> We have [requirement 12.1.2](https://github.com/OWASP/ASVS/blob/master/5.0/en/0x21-V12-Communications.md#v121-general-tls-security-guidance) related to ciphers. Do I understand correctly that if you are using a secure cipher in TLS, that implies secure key exchange with no further thought required?

If you want to have MITM-resistance in TLS, you need to:

* use a not broken TLS versions (12.1.1);
* and use a not broken cipher suite (12.1.2);
* and, when you are the client, check the server's identity (12.3.2);
* and use a not broken group for the (EC)DHE key exchange (do we have an explicit requirement?);
* (and a lots of other things like not using a broken implementation 😄).

> Can you think of another common scenario where a developer will have to handle a situation of manually building an automated key exchange process which seems to be the only case where 11.6.2 would be relevant.

One example, is if you are building an application which features a form of end-to-end encryption between users. I'll try to expand on this if needed (though I am not the best qualified for that) but in this context, maybe it might make sense to allow an end-to-end key exchange which is not resistant to MITM (if we consider the application server to be a MITM).

So my conclusion is that maybe we don't need to change 11.6.2 but instead clarify in a note that this is required for HTTPS/TLS servers as part of another requirement.

@danielcuthbert, @unprovable, @ingo-rauner, any feedback on that?
